2026-09-26 — K11f bounded pidfd progress and unchanged-original replay.
K11f remains OPEN for the historical timeout/final-subject replay; no repair.

The old full run reached seven successful assertions, then its last recycling
loop hit 45 seconds only on EvoCC. The new small reference-built C observer
runs 1024 pipe/fork/SIGSTOP/SIGCONT/reap cycles and checks the already-retired
proc descriptor reports ESRCH on every cycle. No PID sysctl, original source,
original argument, original deadline, kernel or userspace runtime is changed.

Final same-payload GCC/EvoCC pair (seconds for the 1024-cycle loop):
                           reference             subject
root namespace             0.084779              0.351297
fresh PID/mount namespace   0.083400              0.365978
Each side completes both programs and all six assertions, then powers down.
This proves bounded progress and the retired-descriptor check, NOT PID-number
reuse or the original 32768-cycle test, nor a cause for all historic timeouts.

Both root namespaces report pid_max=32768; both fresh namespaces report
4194304. Frozen kernel/pid_namespace.c:110 initializes a new namespace to
PID_MAX_LIMIT, which include/linux/threads.h sets to 4M for this 64-bit profile.
The original test bounds its search by PIDFD_MAX_DEFAULT=32768 and explicitly
skips after a created PID exceeds that value (pidfd_test.c:198-202,364-369).
The root's 32768 value therefore cannot establish that this nested test will
exercise actual reuse of PID 1000. This explains the observed final SKIP,
not the previous execution's 45-second timeout.

Fresh unchanged original, same retained Image/runtime pair, one VM at a time:
  reference: outer PASS, inner 7 PASS / 0 FAIL / 1 SKIP, shutdown at 15.506s
  subject:   outer PASS, inner 7 PASS / 0 FAIL / 1 SKIP, shutdown at 32.966s
The upstream wrapper limit is still 45s; neither the skip nor a successful
retry is relabeled as eight passing assertions or a compiler performance fix.
The older TIMEOUT remains recorded. A new uniform Image must be tested again.

